r we  thought that you know that cuts out your  travel time immediately you get that  help that you need as soon as you need  it and then also being able to integrate  lab services and pharmacy services also  means that you can save more time by  getting care in the comfort of your home  or your office or at school  many people in uganda don't have access  to regular health care some just live  too far away  pandemic related lockdowns made visits  to a doctor even more difficult  and in-person visits also pose the risk  of infection  one of the first confirmed covet cases  in uganda involved a health care worker  in the months that followed many  front-line health workers were infected  most stayed in their jobs but worried  about their families  doctors working from home can help take  the pressure off overcrowded hospitals  and reduce the risk of coronavirus  exposure for doctors patients and their  contacts  it does give you some form of protection  because you're not  you and your client are not in you're  not sharing the same space so if you can  have people stay where they are and you  have this one other doctor at the uh you  know behind a telephone  able to still provide them with the same  services that they would have got if  they actually went to the hospital i  think it benefits both doctors um both  in the telemedicine world and those that  are actually practicing medicine  traditionally  in june 2021 the number of coveted cases  in uganda rose sharply  that same month rocket health recorded  more than 75 thousand telemedicine  consultations  the company also collects laboratory  samples from their clients  and delivers healthcare products and  medicines directly to clients in kampala  and the surrounding region  clients appreciate that they can even  access health care from work  it's convenient and saves time says  sophia nakiranda  the hospital visits like two to three  hours with rocket health i'm going to  make the call  i'll talk to that doctor  in case there is any love that has to be  done they will send someone take the  test as i continue doing my day-to-day  work  davis muzinguzi says less than 10  percent of cases require an in-person  consult  but when the need arises doctors can  send out an ambulance or ask the  patients to come to the company's clinic  the doctor is pleased that his company  is taking off  but the sudden surge in demand for  remote health services during the  pandemic also posed a challenge  we saw our  number of encounters and patients that  we were serving grow by 500 within a  period of one year so it also put a lot  of stress and strain on us to be able to  keep up  with a demand that people are now having  and what we have seen even as the  pandemic starts to subside is that a lot  of people that had that initial  experience with rocket health have  continued to use telemedicine  but for people like davis muzinguzi and  its rocket health company the ideas  tested during the pandemic presented a  business opportunity and helped expand  access to healthcare in uganda  staying in africa we move on to zimbabwe  at the start of the pandemic the  government here banned the operation of  privately owned omnibuses to help  prevent the spread of covid19  only the state-run company could still  transport passengers  dw correspondent privilege musfanhiri  explains what that's meant for many  commuters  here in zimbabwe's capital harare the  lines of people waiting for a bus are  long it's been like this for more than  two years  for student george chiriceri getting to  university every day isn't easy he has  to get a ticket and a seat and steer  clear of the many pickpockets  it's very difficult to get to work on  time or to school  it's very difficult all of the buses  here are expensive  so we're waiting for transportation we  can afford  but there isn't much of that is really  affordable  i got a ticket  he can't always get to his lectures on  time  at the start of the pandemic the  authorities banned private commuter  omnibuses popularly known as combis to  help contain the spread of covet 19.  tino tenda joe also lives in the area he  used to be an assistant in a carpentry  shop after the pandemic hit the  transportation shortage made him late  for work several times so he was let go  now he spends his days by the roadside  hoping to earn a few dollars playing  card games and billiards  about 100 000 jobs were lost due to the  ban on combies  there's little to fill the days for many  of the young people who are now out of  work  some take drugs tinotender joe says  that's not for him  about 20  were grounded by the government's ban  many may never go back in operation  their owners are struggling as are the  drivers and other workers  used to own four buses he had to sell  three of them leaving him with just one  he can no longer operate the ban on  private omnibuses plunged him into  poverty he used to earn at least 240 u.s  dollars a day with his operation  now he's barely getting by working as a  car mechanic  we have to survive somehow  i started selling spare parts from my  bus  when someone asked to buy a part i just  disassembled it from my bus and sold it  before the pandemic i used to repair  combis for other owners but that's over  now too  but things are looking up in early may  the authorities yielded to public  pressure and announced plans to allow  private combies back on the road so  getting to work on time might soon be a  lot easier again  dw science correspondent derek williams  has been keeping you informed about the  latest covid research  this time a viewer asked what happened  to the delta variant has it been  eliminated by omicron  is delta now gone forever and  what do healthcare authorities and  scientists think mutated variants of  covet 19 have have out-competed and  succeeded one another since the  beginning of the pandemic with  a key metric in the equation being  transmissibility  omicron is a lot more infectious than  delta and earlier variants were and  other epidemiological factors being  equal  viruses that are more transmissible than  their peers  can out-compete them  astonishingly quickly infecting the  vulnerable at at such a rate  that slower variants just don't stand  much of a chance um we've seen it three  times already as as this graphic from  from germany's disease control authority  shows um over time  the original virus was replaced almost  completely by the alpha variant remember  alpha which just a few months later lost  out to delta which has now been replaced  by omicron  genetic sequencing has turned up almost  no delta cases in the country since the  end of march and it's not just in  germany  the cdc and the us also no longer  considers delta a variant of concern um  there's  so little of it circulating that in mid  april it was downgraded to what's called  a variant being monitored because  authorities say delta currently doesn't  pose a significant threat to public  health there but the delta variant has  not disappeared completely everywhere in  fact  there are some voices in the research  community that think a delta descendant  might at some point make a comeback  in a recent study an israeli team  tracking variant genomes and waste water  in the city of bioshiva  they've continued to detect delta in  small amounts and hypothesized that if  it remains at current levels  even if they're very low  then there's a chance that as early as  this summer it could possibly surge  again as immunity wanes in the  population  of course  it's impossible to predict the future  but it does seem pretty unlikely  that delta will at some point really  bounce back and and once again pose the  danger  worldwide that it posed back in in 2021  and if one of delta's descendants does  it will likely be  so different from its ancestor variant  that  it'll deserve a designation all its own  [Music]  now we head to south america  a recent study conducted with support  from 