red  these days i can't buy meats with what i  earn from the changas the odd jobs  it's only enough for offal which i cook  for my children  i don't cook the way i used to  everything's got more expensive  meat chicken pasta rice everything  horacio calgado and his family are also  suffering from price increases the  pricey ingredients they see on cooking  shows are rare treats  the costs of food and clothing have  soared since the pandemic so much so  that many middle class argentinians are  also worried  many people worried about falling into  poverty during the pandemic they  wondered how they'd feed their families  if they lost their jobs or what else  they could do to earn money  some came up with new business ideas in  colombia there's a saying nothing sells  better than an empanada felipe abondano  tells us how these savory pastries got  one couples through some hard times  [Music]  the sun isn't up yet in bogota but nelly  coramoto perez is she's already been  busy in the kitchen for two hours  before the pandemic she and her husband  worked in a restaurant in the colombian  capital  but it had to close during lockdown and  they both lost their jobs  the couple only recently emigrated from  venezuela and faced financial ruin  until empanadas saved them  we started during the pandemics  we only had 30 000 pesos left  that's around 7 euros  one night while i was sleeping the idea  came to me  i got up at 3am and told my husband  darling i'm going to sell empanadas this  weekend  now she starts working at 4am every day  her empanada business has become her  biggest source of income  it really helped us get out of our  financial crisis  we can pay our bills and our rent again  that's how it started until things  stabilized  meaning that now i do the selling and my  husband also has a job again  in recent days the rain has made sales  difficult but nelly remains optimistic  she sets up her stand on the street and  before long she has her first customer  the pastry and chicken make these  empanadas rich and quite filling  just like at home that's important  it's important  nelly says her empanada business is one  of the few good things to have come out  of the pandemic  [Music]  and i've made it i've built something up  colombians love their empanadas  during lockdown many missed this popular  street food  so they started buying pre-made frozen  empanadas at the supermarket to heat up  at home  that's been a boon for food producers  like simon ferro  it was a huge challenge for us  we had to increase production and for  that we needed more capital of course  so then we also had to deal with the  financing  we were growing ridiculously fast  especially at discount chains like d1 or  juicedo  the places people shopped at most during  the pandemics  his company bought new machines and  hired more staff  jose attienza found a steady job here  we can make over 50 000 empanadas in  this shift  it depends on the client's order and the  production manager  running three shifts we can produce up  to 120 000 per day  making empanadas seems to be a secure  business model in colombia  whether pre-made or from a street food  stall  empanadas have helped many in colombia  get through the pandemic  and that's all for this show  thanks for watching  please join us again next time and until  then stay safe  [Music]  you

